WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:02.602
-Our first guest tonight
is an Academy Award

00:00:02.602 --> 00:00:04.771
winning actress whose new film
"She Came to Me"

00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:06.806
is in theaters tomorrow,
October 6th.

00:00:06.806 --> 00:00:07.873
Please welcome back to the show,

00:00:07.873 --> 00:00:09.475
the very talented,
our dear friend

00:00:09.475 --> 00:00:11.010
Anne Hathaway, everybody.

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:13.679
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:13.679 --> 00:00:31.997 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:31.997 --> 00:00:34.334
Welcome back, Anne.
-Thank you so much.

00:00:34.334 --> 00:00:36.636
-I'm so happy you're here,
and I want to congratulate you.

00:00:36.636 --> 00:00:39.405
Our lives run in parallel
in many ways.

00:00:39.405 --> 00:00:41.374
We just -- my wife
and I celebrated our 10th.

00:00:41.374 --> 00:00:43.209
You guys just celebrated
your 11th anniversary.

00:00:43.209 --> 00:00:44.543
-Happy anniversary.
-Congratulations.

00:00:44.543 --> 00:00:45.745
-Congratulations to you, too.
-Thank you very much.

00:00:45.745 --> 00:00:48.047
What did you guys do
for the big 11?

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:49.882
-Got rained out.
-You got rained out?

00:00:49.882 --> 00:00:51.951
Was it this...?
-Yes, it was this past Friday?

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:54.787
-Oh, yeah.
That's a terrible day.

00:00:54.787 --> 00:00:57.690
That's literally the skies
sending an omen, yeah.

00:00:57.690 --> 00:01:00.392
-That you should just
let your ambition be dead.

00:01:00.392 --> 00:01:02.895
-Yeah.
-And so we did, we did.

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:05.131
We just kind of like,
re-pajamaed

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:06.366
-Oh, that's nice.

00:01:06.366 --> 00:01:08.033
-I think if you can
re-pajama with someone

00:01:08.033 --> 00:01:10.236
that means that
you've chosen the right mate.

00:01:10.236 --> 00:01:11.270
--You know, I had a moment

00:01:11.270 --> 00:01:12.705
when I was curled up
on his chest,

00:01:12.705 --> 00:01:15.574
and we were watching, like, hour
three of "Abbott Elementary,"

00:01:15.574 --> 00:01:17.577
and I was like,
I'm so happy.

00:01:17.577 --> 00:01:20.446
-There you go.
-This is the right guy for me.

00:01:20.446 --> 00:01:22.181
And I did have that thought that
that I --

00:01:22.181 --> 00:01:23.182
What did you guys do?

00:01:23.182 --> 00:01:25.552
-We, it was September 1st

00:01:25.552 --> 00:01:27.620
and we had --we had
a lovely dinner party

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:29.855
where we all -- it was
a five couples --

00:01:29.855 --> 00:01:32.091
and we all went around
and talked about

00:01:32.091 --> 00:01:34.793
how we met, the day we met.

00:01:34.793 --> 00:01:36.996
And unfortunately,
the last person,

00:01:36.996 --> 00:01:41.333
the last guy who told the story
made it -- was way more romantic

00:01:41.333 --> 00:01:43.302
than the first
four guys had been,

00:01:43.302 --> 00:01:47.540
in a way that we all were like,
"Could we go again?"

00:01:47.540 --> 00:01:49.042
-Actually, I want
to change my answer.

00:01:49.042 --> 00:01:50.443
-I would love to change it.

00:01:50.443 --> 00:01:51.677
I didn't realize you were
allowed to talk like that.

00:01:51.677 --> 00:01:54.213
-You and I met
just for the first time

00:01:54.213 --> 00:01:56.282
just before he and I met
or got together.

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:57.817
-Really?
-Because, yeah,

00:01:57.817 --> 00:01:59.418
in October 2008,

00:01:59.418 --> 00:02:01.387
I hosted "Saturday Night Live"
for the first time.

00:02:01.387 --> 00:02:02.655
-Yeah, I remember well.

00:02:02.655 --> 00:02:04.757
-And then I went out to --
I remember it very well.

00:02:04.757 --> 00:02:07.327
It was one of the most
exciting weeks of my life.

00:02:07.327 --> 00:02:08.794
It still is.

00:02:08.794 --> 00:02:11.497
And then -- and then I went out
to L.A. to work on a movie,

00:02:11.497 --> 00:02:13.165
and that's when Adam
and I got closer.

00:02:13.165 --> 00:02:16.336
And then it was
the North American premiere

00:02:16.336 --> 00:02:17.636
of "Rachel Getting Married,"

00:02:17.636 --> 00:02:19.772
the Jonathan Demme film.
-There you go, Jonathan Demme

00:02:19.772 --> 00:02:21.373
is a great director.
-So that month in my life

00:02:21.373 --> 00:02:22.775
was just crazy.

00:02:22.775 --> 00:02:23.877
-That's so fantastic.

00:02:23.877 --> 00:02:25.344
The other thing I remember,

00:02:25.344 --> 00:02:26.779
The Killers were
the musical guests.

00:02:26.779 --> 00:02:28.514
-Yes.
-And that was a --

00:02:28.514 --> 00:02:30.383
that show also,
I should note,

00:02:30.383 --> 00:02:33.385
was right after
the last writers strike.

00:02:33.385 --> 00:02:34.687
That was, yeah.
-Oh, my gosh.

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:35.988
I forgot about that part.
Wow.

00:02:35.988 --> 00:02:38.424
-And so it was we were
so happy to be back at "SNL"

00:02:38.424 --> 00:02:41.260
and you were such
an exceptional host,

00:02:41.260 --> 00:02:42.895
and so that was really special.

00:02:42.895 --> 00:02:44.864
And it was just a -- it
was just a banger of a show,

00:02:44.864 --> 00:02:46.633
I remember, start to finish.
But my parents were there

00:02:46.633 --> 00:02:48.134
and The Killers
were the musical guests.

00:02:48.134 --> 00:02:51.203
And my parents, I introduced
my parents to The Killers

00:02:51.203 --> 00:02:52.372
who were really nice.

00:02:52.372 --> 00:02:54.206
And as they walked away,
my mom said very loud

00:02:54.206 --> 00:02:55.475
and they definitely heard,
"I don't know why

00:02:55.475 --> 00:02:56.475
they call them The Killers.

00:02:56.475 --> 00:02:57.644
They couldn't be nicer.

00:02:57.644 --> 00:03:00.546
[ Laughter ]

00:03:00.546 --> 00:03:03.916
I'm like, "Well, did you think
they were murderers?"

00:03:03.916 --> 00:03:07.387
-So my mom came to one
of my --

00:03:07.387 --> 00:03:09.055
My little brother
did stand up for a while.

00:03:09.055 --> 00:03:11.190
He's really funny,
and my mom and dad,

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.259
you know,
went to one of his shows.

00:03:13.259 --> 00:03:16.328
And one of the comics
before him

00:03:16.328 --> 00:03:18.698
explained Tinder
to the audience.

00:03:18.698 --> 00:03:19.898
And then in the pause
that followed,

00:03:19.898 --> 00:03:21.934
because most people
knew what Tinder was

00:03:21.934 --> 00:03:23.169
but my mother didn't,

00:03:23.169 --> 00:03:24.904
you just hear
my mom's voice ring out,

00:03:24.904 --> 00:03:26.406
"Oh, that's awful!"

00:03:26.406 --> 00:03:30.242
[ Laughter ]

00:03:30.242 --> 00:03:33.779
-That's what you want to hear
on stage as a comic.

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:37.250
This -- So this film, this is
a beautiful independent film.

00:03:37.250 --> 00:03:39.218
Obviously, there's
a SAG-AFTRA strike.

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:41.688
This is a film with a waiver
that is given

00:03:41.688 --> 00:03:43.889
to some of the independent films
that are not made

00:03:43.889 --> 00:03:46.592
under sort of the auspices
of the struck companies.

00:03:46.592 --> 00:03:48.194
Is it nice to be able
to come up

00:03:48.194 --> 00:03:50.362
and talk about sort of
one of these smaller films

00:03:50.362 --> 00:03:52.799
that sometimes don't get
the same amount of attention?

00:03:52.799 --> 00:03:55.001
-Yeah.
Yeah, it's amazing.

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:56.535
I mean, for a lot of reasons.

00:03:56.535 --> 00:04:00.406
One, Rebecca Miller is
our writer and our director,

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:02.140
and I think she's been
working on this project

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:03.476
for close to a decade.

00:04:03.476 --> 00:04:04.844
-That's amazing.
-It's amazing.

00:04:04.844 --> 00:04:07.680
And she is the most brilliant,
talented person.

00:04:07.680 --> 00:04:09.815
She's not just a director
and a writer,

00:04:09.815 --> 00:04:11.050
but she's also an artist.

00:04:11.050 --> 00:04:14.486
She's a painter,
and this is, the movie,

00:04:14.486 --> 00:04:18.024
the screenplay is based
on a short story that she wrote

00:04:18.024 --> 00:04:19.725
and then adapted herself
and then transformed.

00:04:19.725 --> 00:04:21.394
It's all set
in the world of opera,

00:04:21.394 --> 00:04:23.796
but it's also set
in the world of tugboats.

00:04:23.796 --> 00:04:25.565
-That's --
-Which I know we've all

00:04:25.565 --> 00:04:26.799
seen that movie a million times.
-It's such a bummer

00:04:26.799 --> 00:04:28.368
because I have that idea
and I'm now like, huh.

00:04:28.368 --> 00:04:29.668
-Did you?

00:04:29.668 --> 00:04:31.804
I'm like, do it, do it before
someone beats you to it.

00:04:31.804 --> 00:04:34.039
-It's that thing
where I love --

00:04:34.039 --> 00:04:37.176
I love going to -- I love --
I love all kinds of movies.

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:39.112
You know, I'm not just like --
but what I think --

00:04:39.112 --> 00:04:41.713
when I think that
we're at our best as an industry

00:04:41.713 --> 00:04:43.082
and when I think
we're as our best,

00:04:43.082 --> 00:04:45.685
like as an audience is
when we have different options.

00:04:45.685 --> 00:04:47.419
And I'm just really proud to be,
I don't know,

00:04:47.419 --> 00:04:50.556
part of an independent offering
that's totally original,

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:52.258
that's a little bit
like a French movie,

00:04:52.258 --> 00:04:53.859
but without subtitles
and in English.

00:04:53.859 --> 00:04:55.328
-That's very nice.
-[ Laughs ]

00:04:55.328 --> 00:04:57.063
-Every time I watch
a French movie, I'm like,

00:04:57.063 --> 00:04:58.197
"Couldn't you done it easy?

00:04:58.197 --> 00:04:59.732
Couldn't you made it
easier for me?"

00:04:59.732 --> 00:05:01.133
-For me, for me.

00:05:01.133 --> 00:05:03.035
-Now I can't even look
at my phone during this movie.

00:05:03.035 --> 00:05:04.971
-How rude.
-You --

00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:08.274
and you auditioned
for Rebecca Miller years ago.

00:05:08.274 --> 00:05:10.209
-I was a literal teenager.
-Wow.

00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:11.778
And I auditioned for --
I auditioned

00:05:11.778 --> 00:05:13.879
for her a week before I met you.

00:05:13.879 --> 00:05:15.481
No, I auditioned --

00:05:15.481 --> 00:05:16.715
-I remember you were
always like you

00:05:16.715 --> 00:05:17.816
I'm so excited.

00:05:17.816 --> 00:05:19.251
I just auditioned
for Rebecca Miller.

00:05:19.251 --> 00:05:21.053
-I was 19 years old,

00:05:21.053 --> 00:05:23.089
and she was making
an incredible movie

00:05:23.089 --> 00:05:24.556
called "The Ballad
of Jack and Rose."

00:05:24.556 --> 00:05:26.225
And I was going to Vassar
at the time,

00:05:26.225 --> 00:05:28.428
and I was taking
a romantic poetry course.

00:05:28.428 --> 00:05:30.563
And I remember going into
the audition,

00:05:30.563 --> 00:05:32.665
and you know how it is.
You're like one fish

00:05:32.665 --> 00:05:35.568
in a sea of an ocean
of actresses.

00:05:35.568 --> 00:05:37.537
And I didn't know if I was
going to get the part,

00:05:37.537 --> 00:05:39.104
but I just wanted to let
her know what the screenplay

00:05:39.104 --> 00:05:41.473
had meant to me because
I really love her writing.

00:05:41.473 --> 00:05:45.177
And I just handed her a poem
that reminded me

00:05:45.177 --> 00:05:47.947
of the screenplay
that she'd written.

00:05:47.947 --> 00:05:50.383
And so when I met her,
she was actually my last meeting

00:05:50.383 --> 00:05:52.785
before the pandemic kicked in.
-Oh, wow.

00:05:52.785 --> 00:05:54.420
-And I walked in and she goes,
"It's really nice to meet you."

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:56.456
And I said,
"Well, technically we've met."

00:05:56.456 --> 00:05:59.324
And I remind her,
and she didn't remember me.

00:05:59.324 --> 00:06:00.559
Hopefully my acting
has improved since then,

00:06:00.559 --> 00:06:02.427
but -- but she did remember
the poem.

00:06:02.427 --> 00:06:06.031
-Oh, and that's very sweet
to have a poem remembered.

00:06:06.031 --> 00:06:07.166
She's like,
In all my years,

00:06:07.166 --> 00:06:09.102
no one had
the nerve to hand me a poem.

00:06:09.102 --> 00:06:12.070
-The nerve -- the nerve
or the nerd?

00:06:12.070 --> 00:06:13.472
-It's a little, I think,
it's a combo of both

00:06:13.472 --> 00:06:15.241
when you give someone a poem.
-A nervy nerd.

00:06:15.241 --> 00:06:16.442
-[ Laughs ]
-That seems lovely.

00:06:16.442 --> 00:06:18.544
-This is -- you're also a
producer on this film.

00:06:18.544 --> 00:06:20.913
-I am.
-And I'm wondering, like,

00:06:20.913 --> 00:06:23.015
how, you know,
because again,

00:06:23.015 --> 00:06:24.884
you're this wonderful actor.

00:06:24.884 --> 00:06:26.585
And then do you also
have to bring that in?

00:06:26.585 --> 00:06:27.619
And this is what I also love.

00:06:27.619 --> 00:06:28.955
This is such a great
New York cast.

00:06:28.955 --> 00:06:30.523
Marisa Tomei and Peter Dinklage.

00:06:30.523 --> 00:06:32.691
This is a real East Coast
posse right here.

00:06:32.691 --> 00:06:34.993
And do you ever
as a producer go,

00:06:34.993 --> 00:06:37.497
hey, maybe a little bit
more like this or do you...?

00:06:37.497 --> 00:06:39.165
-What do you mean?
-Like, do you ever give

00:06:39.165 --> 00:06:41.233
your other actors like,
hey, I just want to remind you

00:06:41.233 --> 00:06:42.567
that I'm also, you know?
-Oh, yeah.

00:06:42.567 --> 00:06:44.103
-Yeah, good. Yeah.
-I have to.

00:06:44.103 --> 00:06:45.904
-You got to, right?
You care about the product.

00:06:45.904 --> 00:06:47.973
Your name's out there.
-I just don't know that

00:06:47.973 --> 00:06:50.109
it's been a complete experience
since I've crushed the spirit

00:06:50.109 --> 00:06:51.543
of the person that I'm with.

00:06:51.543 --> 00:06:53.145
You know, that's just kind of --
What?

00:06:53.145 --> 00:06:55.314
[ Laughter ]

00:06:55.314 --> 00:06:57.383
I am kidding.
I promise.

00:06:57.383 --> 00:06:59.117
-You should give
all your notes from here

00:06:59.117 --> 00:07:01.087
on out via poem
and just be like,

00:07:01.087 --> 00:07:02.455
I have something to say
about your acting,

00:07:02.455 --> 00:07:04.390
but I would like you
to read a poem.

00:07:04.390 --> 00:07:06.859
-Okay. So if I give --
I'm going to try this.

00:07:06.859 --> 00:07:08.728
-Okay, good.

00:07:08.728 --> 00:07:10.730
-Or I'm going to deny this.
-Oh!

00:07:10.730 --> 00:07:12.564
-I don't know
if I can fly this,

00:07:12.564 --> 00:07:14.533
but I'm going to try this.

00:07:14.533 --> 00:07:16.168
-This is like a magic trick,
and I don't like it.

00:07:16.168 --> 00:07:17.937
[ Laughter ]

00:07:17.937 --> 00:07:19.538
I don't like how quickly
you're rhyming.

00:07:19.538 --> 00:07:21.573
[ Laughs ]

00:07:21.573 --> 00:07:23.609
-If you don't like it --
No, I'm just gonna stop.

00:07:23.609 --> 00:07:25.177
-Okay, good.
I have more questions

00:07:25.177 --> 00:07:26.712
to ask you, and we're going
to show a clip of the movie.

00:07:26.712 --> 00:07:28.114
Right back with more
from Anne Hathaway.

